<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Icelandic Commonwealth</h1><p class="st-meta">930 CE – 1262 CE · capital Þingvellir</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Medieval Iceland's kingless republic of chieftains and law assemblies, the society that produced the Icelandic sagas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlers, mostly from western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and the Norse colonies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>, occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-iceland">Iceland</a> from about 870, a period recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landnamabok">Landnámabók</a>. Around 930 they founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/althing">Althing</a>, a general assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thingvellir">Þingvellir</a>, which gave the island a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> without a king.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Power rested with chieftains, the goðar, whose followers could choose which chieftain to support. A law-speaker recited the law at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/althing">Althing</a>, and courts settled disputes, though there was no executive to enforce verdicts. In 1000 the Althing, guided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thorgeir-ljosvetningagodi">Þorgeir Ljósvetningagoði</a>, adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The Commonwealth produced the sagas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Eddas</a> and the histories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 13th century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sturlung-age">Sturlung Age</a>, a few great families fought for supremacy, and the Norwegian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon IV</a> intervened. In 1262-1264 the Icelanders accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-covenant-iceland">Old Covenant</a>, submitting to Norwegian rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gissur-thorvaldsson">Gissur Þorvaldsson</a> became earl. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-iceland">Iceland</a> later passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and regained independence as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-iceland">Kingdom of Iceland</a> and Republic of Iceland.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/icelandic-commonwealth.jpg" alt="Icelandic Commonwealth" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: Colovia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Icelandic_Commonwealth_Quarters.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>930 CE – 1262 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Þingvellir</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Þorgeir Ljósvetningagoði, Snorri Sturluson, Gissur Þorvaldsson</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism, Christianity from 1000</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse (Old Icelandic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iceland">Iceland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Icelandic Free State, Þjóðveldið</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1096&amp;focus=icelandic-commonwealth" aria-label="Icelandic Commonwealth on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4322 50 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Icelandic Commonwealth lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4642 306L5225 425"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4642 306h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4642 306h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1096&amp;focus=icelandic-commonwealth">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
